The '''2013–14 season''' was the 120th season of competitive football by [[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]]. Celtic had a new shirt sponsor for the season with Irish Cider brand [[Magners]] taking over from [[Tennent's Lager]] for the next three years.<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/celtic/9791057/Celtic-reveal-costs-of-reconstruction-after-unveiling-three-year-shirt-sponsorship-deal-with-Magners.html|title= Celtic reveal costs of reconstruction after unveiling three-year shirt sponsorship deal with Magners|date=9 June 2013|work= The Daily Telegraph|access-date=21 August 2013 }}</ref>

Celtic began the season with further Champions League success in their sights. They were faced with the burden of replacing three main pieces of their squad in; [[Gary Hooper]] and [[Kelvin Wilson]] were sold to [[Norwich City]] and [[Nottingham Forest]] respectively, whilst the highly rated [[Victor Wanyama]] was sold to [[Southampton F.C.|Southampton]] for a Scottish record fee of £12.5 million.<ref>{{cite web|title=Celtic in record breaking transfer sale as Wanyama heads south|url=http://www.scotzine.com/2013/07/celtic-in-record-breaking-transfer-sale-as-wanyama-heads-south/|publisher=Scotzine|access-date=7 May 2014|date=11 July 2013}}</ref> Neil Lennon rebuilt his squad by signing [[Virgil van Dijk]] and [[Amido Balde]] in the summer, and [[Teemu Pukki]] from [[Schalke 04]] on transfer deadline day.<ref>{{cite web|title=Celtic sign Teemu Pukki from Schalke on four-year deal|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/23832031|publisher=BBC Sport|access-date=17 February 2014|date=31 August 2013}}</ref> Celtic were drawn with [[Cliftonville]] in their first qualifying round and easily disposed of the Northern Irish champions 5–0 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite web|title=Celtic 2 Cliftonville 0|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/23324257|publisher=BBC Sport|access-date=17 February 2014|date=23 July 2013}}</ref> They were next drawn with [[IF Elfsborg]] and squeezed by with a 1–0 aggregate win, with [[Kris Commons]] scoring the only goal in the first leg at [[Celtic Park]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Campbell|first=Andy|title=Elfsborg 0 Celtic 0|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/23513157|publisher=BBC Sport|access-date=17 February 2014|date=7 August 2013}}</ref> Although favoured to qualify for the group stages with minimal challenge after being drawn with Kazakh champions [[Shakhter Karagandy]], Celtic found themselves losing the first leg in [[Karagandy]] 2–0.<ref>{{cite web|last=Grant|first=Michael|title=Shakhter Karagandy 2 Celtic 0: stirring cocktail leaves Lennon's side groggy|url=http://www.heraldscotland.com/sport/football/shakhter-karagandy-2-celtic-0-stirring-cocktail-leaves-lennon-s-side-groggy.1377061269|publisher=Herald Scotland|access-date=17 February 2014|date=21 August 2013}}</ref> Celtic returned to Scotland to an electric atmosphere at Celtic Park in the home leg, in proved to be the high point of their 2013-14 Champions League campaign. They overcame the 2–0 deficit to level the aggregate thanks to goals by Kris Commons and [[Georgios Samaras]]. [[James Forrest (footballer, born 1991)|James Forrest]] completed the comeback for Celtic with a 90th-minute strike, sending Celtic Park into a frenzy.<ref>{{cite web|last=Lindsay|first=Clive|title=Celtic 3 Shakhter Karagandy 0|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/23776602|publisher=BBC Sport|access-date=17 February 2014|date=28 August 2013}}</ref>

Thereafter, Celtic found themselves drawn with [[AC Milan]], [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]] and [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] (again) in the group stages.<ref>{{cite web|title=Barcelona, Milan, Ajax and Celtic drawn together |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0250-0c50fddbac4f-7d343c85c61d-1000--barcelona-milan-ajax-and-celtic-drawn-together/|publisher=[[UEFA]]|access-date=17 February 2014|date=29 August 2013}}</ref> The campaign was dismal; winning only once with a 2–1 home win over Ajax,<ref>{{cite web|last=Lamont|first=Alasdair|title=Celtic 2 Ajax 1|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/24614436|publisher=BBC Sport|access-date=17 February 2014|date=22 October 2013}}</ref> and slumping to a 6–1 rout away against Barcelona in the final group match.<ref name="thrash">{{cite web|last=Lamont|first=Alasdair|title=Barcelona 6 Celtic 1 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25303700|publisher=BBC Sport|access-date=17 February 2014|date=11 December 2013}}</ref> That game saw Celtic concede the most goals they had ever done in a single European tie, and equalled their previous heaviest defeat in Europe (5–0 against [[FC Artmedia Bratislava]]).<ref name="thrash" />

Celtic's 2014 domestic campaign was filled with ups and downs. Towards the end of February, Celtic had not lost a single game in the league,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Southwick |first1=Andrew |title=Aberdeen 2-1 Celtic |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/25892139 |access-date=22 January 2021 |work=BBC Sport |date=25 February 2014}}</ref> and drew just twice against [[Dundee United F.C.|Dundee United]] and [[Hibernian F.C.|Hibernian]]. Their league success did not translate to cup competition however; they bowed out of both the [[Scottish League Cup]] (0–1 to [[Greenock Morton F.C.|Morton]]),<ref>{{cite web|title=Celtic 0 - 1 Morton |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/24122182|publisher=BBC Sport|access-date=17 March 2014|date=24 September 2014}}</ref> and the [[Scottish Cup]](1–2 to [[Aberdeen]]),<ref>{{cite web|last=MacDonald|first=Calum|title=Celtic 1 - 2 Aberdeen|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25995228|access-date=17 March 2014|date=8 February 2014}}</ref> both at [[Celtic Park]]. Celtic also struggled to find a suitable strike partner for [[Anthony Stokes]], forcing [[Kris Commons]] into a striking role. Neil Lennon signed former Hibs striker [[Leigh Griffiths]] on [[Transfer window|transfer deadline day]] in January 2014,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25977707|title=Wolves striker Griffiths joins Celtic|publisher=BBC Sport|date=31 January 2014}}</ref> as well as [[Stefan Johansen]] earlier in the month.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.scotsman.com/sport/football/spfl/stefan-johansen-signs-for-celtic-1-3268643|last=Walker|first=Mark|title=Stefan Johansen signs for Celtic|publisher=The Scotsman |date=15 January 2014 |access-date=15 January 2014}}</ref> After being unable to reach an agreement on a new contract, [[Joe Ledley]] left Celtic for [[Crystal Palace F.C.|Crystal Palace]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Ledley Joins Palace |url=https://www.cpfc.co.uk/news/2014/january/ledley-joins-palace/ |work=Crystal Palace FC |date=31 January 2014}}</ref>

On 2 February 2014 goalkeeper Fraser Forster set a new a club-record of 11 league clean sheets in a row,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/26011023 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=3 February 2014 |title=Celtic: Neil Lennon applauds record-breaking Fraser Forster}}</ref> surpassing a record of 10 clean sheets set by [[Charlie Shaw (footballer born 1885)|Charlie Shaw]] in the 1921–22 season.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www1.skysports.com/football/news/11787/9147994/neil-lennon-proud-to-break-92-year-old-record-in-win-over-st-mirren |title=Neil Lennon proud to break 92-year-old record in win over St Mirren  |access-date=3 February 2014}}</ref> On 22 February, he broke [[Bobby Clark (footballer, born 1945)|Bobby Clark]]'s [[Scottish League]] record of 1155 minutes without conceding a goal in a league match. Celtic won 2–0 away at Hearts, and Forster racked up his 13th consecutive clean sheet in the league.<ref>{{cite web|title=Scottish Premiership LIVE: Hearts v Celtic |url=http://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/sport/football/football-match-reports/scottish-premiership-live-hearts-v-3172172 |publisher=Daily Record |access-date=22 February 2014 |date=22 February 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140425003512/http://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/sport/football/football-match-reports/scottish-premiership-live-hearts-v-3172172 |archive-date=25 April 2014 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Hearts 0 Celtic 2: Double thumbs up for Celtic at Tynecastle as Fraser Forster sets new record and Leigh Griffiths strikes |url=http://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/sport/football/football-match-reports/hearts-0-celtic-2-double-3172864 |publisher=Daily Record |access-date=1 March 2014 |date=22 February 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140309102535/http://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/sport/football/football-match-reports/hearts-0-celtic-2-double-3172864 |archive-date= 9 March 2014 }}</ref> 
Forster's clean sheet run finally ended on 1,256 minutes against [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]] on 25 February 2014, as Aberdeen defeated Celtic 2–1 to end their unbeaten run in the league.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25892139 |publisher=BBC |access-date=26 February 2014 |title=BBC Sport - Aberdeen 2-1 Celtic}}</ref>

Celtic finished the season as league champions again, their third consecutive [[Scottish Professional Football League|Scottish League]] title,<ref>{{cite web|last=Campbell|first=Alan|title=Celtic crush Partick Thistle to make it three SPL titles in a row|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/mar/26/celtic-partick-thistle-scottish-premier-league-match-report|work=The Guardian|access-date=28 April 2014|date=26 March 2014}}</ref> scoring 102 goals in the process.<ref>{{cite web|title=Celtic 3 Dundee Utd 1: champion Hoops take home the trophy|url=http://www.heraldscotland.com/sport/football/celtic-3-dundee-utd-1-celtic-confirm-champion-status-with-united-win-and-take-home-th.1399815846|publisher=Herald Online|access-date=12 May 2014|date=11 May 2014}}</ref> Celtic clinched the title after a 5–1 away win against [[Partick Thistle F.C.|Partick Thistle]] on 26 March 2014.<ref>{{cite web|url = https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/mar/26/celtic-partick-thistle-scottish-premier-league-match-report|title=Celtic crush Partick Thistle to make it three SPL titles in a row|date=26 March 2014|work=The Guardian|access-date=26 March 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url = https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/celtic/10724728/Partick-Thistle-1-Celtic-5-match-report.html|title=Partick Thistle 1 Celtic 5|date=26 March 2014|work=The Daily Telegraph|access-date=26 March 2014}}</ref>
It is the earliest that the title has been won since the 1928–29 season, when [[Old Firm]] Rivals [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]] won it on 16 March.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.thescore.ie/celtic-title-1383544-Mar2014/|title=Stokes leads the way as five-goal Celtic win 45th Scottish title|date=26 March 2014|work=The Score|access-date=26 March 2014|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140327234236/http://www.thescore.ie/celtic-title-1383544-Mar2014/|archive-date=27 March 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url = http://www.independent.ie/sport/soccer/celtic-make-it-three-titles-in-a-row-with-victory-over-partick-thistle-30129170.html|title=Celtic make it three titles in a row with victory over Partick Thistle|date=26 March 2014|work=Irish Independent|access-date=26 March 2014}}</ref>

Kris Commons was the top scorer in Scotland, netting 32 goals, and won both the [[PFA Scotland Players' Player of the Year|PFA Scotland]] and [[SFWA Footballer of the Year|Scottish Football Writers']] Player of the Year awards.<ref>{{cite web|title=Celtic's Kris Commons named Premiership player of year|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/27183434|publisher=BBC Sport|date=28 April 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Celtic's Kris Commons is football writers' player of year|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/27362516|publisher=BBC Sport|access-date=12 May 2014|date=11 May 2014}}</ref>

